2. Raspberry Pi OS and Debian packages
--------------------------------------

The rest of the system comes from Debian's and Raspberry Pi's packages,
installed unmodified from the Debian archive (https://deb.debian.org/debian)
and the Raspberry Pi archive (https://archive.raspberrypi.com/debian): the
Linux kernel, the firmware, Qt, SDL2, mpv and libmpv, FFmpeg, Chromium,
Python and everything else. Each package keeps its own copyright and
licence, in /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright on the image, and the GNU
licences many of them use are in /usr/share/common-licenses. Which
packages, at which versions, the image says itself: `dpkg-query -W` on it.

Source code. Each package's source, at the version on the image, is in the
same archives: `apt-get source <package>` once deb-src lines are added to
the image's apt sources, and https://snapshot.debian.org for Debian's
earlier versions. The Raspberry Pi kernel's is also at
https://github.com/raspberrypi/linux.

In addition, for three years after an image is published, OSD/OS will give
anyone who asks a complete machine-readable copy of the source code of the
GPL and LGPL licensed software on that image, for no more than the cost of
providing it. Ask at https://github.com/mehmetraif/OSD-OS/issues, naming
the image.


3. What OSD/OS's stage adds
---------------------------

- yt-dlp (https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp), from its nightly builds, in
  the app's data directory (~/.local/share/OSD-OS/bin/yt-dlp), from where
  it replaces itself with newer builds. It is released into the public
  domain (The Unlicense).

- Deno (https://deno.com), /usr/local/bin/deno, under the MIT License,
  Copyright 2018-2026 the Deno authors. The licence's text is in
  /usr/local/share/doc/deno/LICENSE.md.

- FinalBurn Neo (https://github.com/libretro/FBNeo), the arcade emulator
  RetroArch plays the Arcade module's games with for RetroAchievements and
  online play: the core /usr/local/lib/libretro/fbneo_libretro.so, from
  libretro's nightly builds (https://buildbot.libretro.com), with its list
  of arcade games in /usr/local/share/libretro/fbneo/. It is Team FB Neo's,
  under its own licence, which also brings in MAME's: it may be used and
  shared, never sold, and a project that uses it may not ask for
  donations. The licence's full text is in
  /usr/local/share/doc/fbneo/license.txt, and the build's date and CRC in
  /usr/local/share/doc/fbneo/BUILD. No ROM images come with it.
  RetroArch itself is Debian's retroarch package (GPL-3.0).

- Widevine (the libwidevinecdm0 package), Google's content decryption
  module, which the Netflix and Prime Video players need. It is installed
  from the Raspberry Pi archive, as Raspberry Pi OS installs it. It is
  Google's proprietary software: none of the licences in this file cover
  it, and its package carries no licence of its own.

An image built with OSDOS_YOUTUBE=0 has neither yt-dlp nor Deno, one built
with OSDOS_STREAMING=0 has no Chromium or Widevine, and one built with
OSDOS_ARCADE=0 has no FinalBurn Neo.


4. Data the modules show
------------------------

- This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by TMDB
  (https://www.themoviedb.org). The Netflix and Prime Video modules'
  catalogues come from it, with JustWatch's listings of where a title
  streams through it.
- Weather data by Open-Meteo.com (https://open-meteo.com), under the
  Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0).
- A title's page on a service comes from Wikidata (https://www.wikidata.org),
  whose data is in the public domain (CC0).


